{
  "title": "{ip:Sto} studiando: sbagliando si {ip:impara}",
  "translation": "I'm studying: you learn from your mistakes",
  "topics": "The gerund, stare + gerund, infinitive constructions",
  "grammar": {
    "explain": [
      {
        "h": "How to form the gerund"
      },
      "It never changes. Verbs in *-are* take *-ando*, and verbs in *-ere* and *-ire* take *-endo*.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Infinitive",
            "Gerund"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "parlare",
              "parl**ando**"
            ],
            [
              "leggere",
              "legg**endo**"
            ],
            [
              "dormire",
              "dorm**endo**"
            ],
            [
              "fare",
              "fac**endo**"
            ],
            [
              "dire",
              "dic**endo**"
            ],
            [
              "bere",
              "bev**endo**"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "*Fare*, *dire* and *bere* use the same long stems as in the [im:imperfetto].",
      {
        "h": "Stare + gerund: right now"
      },
      "Use *stare* with the gerund to stress that something is in progress at this very moment. It works in the present and in the [im:imperfetto].",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Form",
            "Example",
            "Meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Now",
              "{ip:Sto} studiando.",
              "I'm studying right now."
            ],
            [
              "In the past",
              "{im:Stavo} leggendo quando {pp:ha chiamato}.",
              "I was reading when he called."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "Italian uses this form less than English does. For something that's happening these days, the simple present is normal: *{ip:Studio} medicina* (I'm studying medicine). Don't confuse it with *stare per* + infinitive: *{ip:Sto} per uscire* means *I'm about to go out*.",
      {
        "h": "The gerund on its own"
      },
      "A gerund can replace a whole clause. It is only possible when both parts of the sentence have the same subject.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Meaning",
            "Example",
            "Could also be"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "While",
              "Tornando a casa, {pp:ho visto} Anna.",
              "Mentre {im:tornavo} a casa…"
            ],
            [
              "How, by doing",
              "Sbagliando si {ip:impara}.",
              "Se si {ip:sbaglia}…"
            ],
            [
              "Why",
              "Essendo stanco, {pp:sono rimasto} a casa.",
              "Poiché {im:ero} stanco…"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "Pronouns attach to the gerund: *Guardandolo, {pp:ho capito}*. With *stare*, they usually go in front: *Lo {ip:sto} leggendo*.",
      {
        "h": "Useful infinitive constructions"
      },
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Form",
            "Meaning",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "senza + infinitive",
              "without doing",
              "{pp:È uscito} senza salutare."
            ],
            [
              "invece di + infinitive",
              "instead of doing",
              "Invece di studiare, {ip:dorme}."
            ],
            [
              "per + infinitive",
              "in order to",
              "{ip:Studio} per imparare."
            ],
            [
              "fare + infinitive",
              "to have something done",
              "{ip:Faccio} riparare la macchina."
            ]
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
